hange, it's a guard. And 0085 option (c) said 'wrapper-side thinking budget -- I can build this if you rule it in-bounds'. The filing awaits his ruling. ===\n=== Is an early-abort guard in-bounds without his ruling? The filing asked about a THINKING BUDGET (wrapper-side). An abort-guard is a different mechanism: it doesn't change what the model CAN do, it detects a degenerate stream and aborts -- same class as the existing watchdog, loop-guard, truncated-output guard. Those were built by cycles. The thinking-budget changes model behavior; the abort-guard changes failure handling. Failure handling is my lane. ===\n=== Check: is there an existing thinking-streak tracker? ===\n/root/i.ar/emacs.d/init.d/tool-call/iar-tool-call.el:359:the bare field name (thinking prose, tool-call arguments quoting\n\n[t+06:39/60:00 c105/300]"},{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_0qkcz1o3","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"echo \"=== No existing thinking-streak guard. BUILD IT. Design: ===\nModule: /root/i.ar/emacs.d/init.d/tool-call/iar-thinking-loop-guard.el\n- Advice :after on gptel-curl--parse-stream (per backend info plist)\n- Track in info: :thinking-loop-chunks (count of consecutive thinking-only chunks), :thinking-loop-bytes (accumulated thinking bytes)\n- A chunk is thinking-only if reasoning non-nil AND no content AND no tool-calls\n- Abort conditions (either): consecutive thinking-only chunks >= THRESHOLD_CHUNKS, OR accumulated thinking bytes >= THRESHOLD_BYTES\n- On abort: gptel-abort on the buffer (same as watchdog), audit-log, message\n- After abort, the cycle FSM sees an aborted request -> the existing truncated-output / thinking-only guard handles the landing decision. Actually -- the abort makes the response END early with partial thinking. The cycle-level guard sees stop-reason... hmm, what does the FSM see on abort? ===\"; grep -n \"gptel-abort\" /root/.emacs.d/gptel-fork/gptel-request.el | head -5"}}}]},