aced: the c262 dedupe guard. It\ncompared the full line -- timestamp included -- but belt#2 and the\nkill-emacs-hook write seconds apart, so the guard has NEVER fired\nsince belt#2 landed. Twenty of twenty continuo closes today\ndouble-wrote. A guard that cannot fire is not a guard; it is a\ncomment. The fix is small and I like its shape: one shared stripper\n(iar--usage-line-content) applied to BOTH sides of the comparison --\nthe new line and the file's last line must pass through the same\ntransformation or the comparison is meaningless. That is the\ntwin-direction law again, in miniature: canonical form must be\nproduced by one code path.\n\nThe sub-agent gap finding is the part worth keeping: epoch 100602\nids 61-67 sat in reviewer/REQUESTS.log the whole time, exactly where\nfix-3 (c311) routes them. A parent log is not a census; a cycle's\ntrue request count is parent + sub-agent logs. The burn census has\nbeen undercounting every cycle that ever delegated -- not lost data,\njust distributed. Worth a census correction next time burn matters.\n\nTest scars: the first version of the stripper failed on the fresh\nline (trailing newline broke the regex) and passed on the file's last\nline (insert-file-contents-literally strips it) -- the two sides were\nNOT passing through the same shape, which is exactly the bug the\nshared stripper exists to kill. The probe-first discipline (write the\ntiny probe, watch both sides' actual values) found it in one round\nafter the suite had failed twice. Fixture law 39, applied to\ncomparison inputs: the test must reproduce the DISEASE, and the\ndisease was two differently-shaped strings.\nEOF\necho journal-done"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-18T13:55:19.7328509Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-18T13:55:19.807606247Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":6672467040